AN UGLY TRUTH by Sheera Frenkel and Cecilia Kang

2021. Harper/Bridge Street Press.  Paperback.

I keep telling myself that I need to read a good insider's book on
Facebook, and I'm proud to say that i finally have. I was inspired to
read this by seeing Aaron Sorkin's second Facebook movie (with no
David Fincher this time) "The Social Reckoning" which I will review
separately. When it was time to pick a book off the shelf, I had a
choice of two anti-Facebook reads, "An Ugly Truth" or "Zucked".
I went with An Ugly Truth, a 2021 book from two New York Times
journalists, Sheera Frenkel (Cybersecurity, San Francisco Silicon
Valley) and Cecilia Kang (Regulatory, Washington DC).

I typically enjoy books by journalists because they know how to write
and they know how to keep you interested (provided the subject matter
itself is of suitable interest of course). This book is a good example
of that.

Frenkel/Kang handle both the Silicon Valley and Washington DC sides of
Facebook's operations for a period covering six months before and
after Donald Trump's first term as President (2016-2020). Advertising
and Trolling from Russia's IRA ("Internet Research Agency", nothing to
do with Northern Ireland) is the first main scandal as Facebook are
shown never to have taken security seriously enough. Agitation against
the Rohingya in Burma is the major international Facebook incident
covered in the book. Later in the book the Cambridge Analytica data
steal and COVID and January 6 2021 are the prominent issues.

There are passing references to other Facebook scandals, such as
them running mood experiments over their users ("Facebook treats users
like Lab Rats"), addictive behaviour to teenagers and having to pay
for news content in Australia; but the main argument of the book is
Facebook prioritised growth and hit behind an absolutist Free Speech
position and especially unfettered free speech for politicians.

The book doesn't trouble itself with anti-Democrat sentiment; Trump's
Facebook ad-spend is quoted without telling how much Hilary Clinton's
campaign or Joe Biden's campaign spent on the platform. Facebook is
said to be the second largest lobbyist in Washington DC, the largest
lobbyist is left a mystery. Small omissions like this are frustrating
and show if not outright bias at least the political leanings of the
New York Times authors of this book. The Black Lives Matter movement
and George Floyd protests are presented in a flattering light, which
might be more a product of the book's 2020-2021 writing period when
BLM could do no wrong.

Especially good in this book is the coverage of Sheryl Sandberg, and
how her power at Facebook waxes then wanes as Zuckerberg reasserts
control.

Facebook's efforts to start a cryptocurrency (which seem to have come
to nothing) are mentioned here and a few other projects that failed.
They changed their name to Meta in 2021 a few months after this book
was published. The virutal reality headset Facebook world seems to
also have come to nothing, but as people turn off traditional media in
drovers we are all still living in Facebook's Metaverse.

